// probe.cpp — A MEASUREMENT INSTRUMENT. It is NOT part of the shipped kernel.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (spec §4.5, current_state.md §5 task 1). Persistent naming is the #1 risk in
// the project, and the spec is explicit that our knowledge of it is second-hand: OCCT's
// `Generated`/`Modified`/`IsDeleted` history is "robust for faces but historically weakest for edges
// and vertices generated by boolean section curves". That sentence is a citation, not a measurement.
// Before designing the naming resolver we find out what OCCT 7.9.3 ACTUALLY reports, on the shapes
// Bunyan actually needs: a cylinder, a boolean, a fillet, and the hardest case of all — a fillet on
// an edge that a boolean created.
//
// It links as its own WASM module so that the shipped kernel stays clean until the measurements say
// what to build. It prints; it does not name. Nothing here is on the identity path.
//
// ⚠ ONE DELIBERATE VIOLATION OF THE PROJECT'S OWN RULE, AND IT IS CONFINED TO THIS FILE.
// The naming rule (D1) is that identity NEVER comes from geometry. This probe labels sub-shapes
// geometrically (centroid, area, kind of surface) — because a measurement instrument's job is to
// describe what came out so a human can read it. That is the opposite of assigning identity from it.
// No output of this file is consumed by the kernel; it is consumed by a person.

